# [DIR] §4 Computer-assisted proofs, §4.1 Dyadic partitioning

This file was automatically generated by Claude.

Source [DIR]: P. Durcik, P. Ivanisvili, J. Roos, *Sharp isoperimetric inequalities on the Hamming
cube near the critical exponent*, arXiv:2407.12674v2, §4 (introductory paragraph) and §4.1.

## Notation

A rectangle $[a,b]=[a_1,b_1]\times\dots\times[a_n,b_n]\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ is `Set.Icc a b` for
`a b : Fin n → ℝ` (with the componentwise order). For $n=1,2$ we provide the specializations
`IsTightLowerBound₁`, `IsTightLowerBound₂` with lower bounds written
$\underline f(\underline x,\overline x)$, resp.
$\underline f(\underline x_1,\overline x_1,\underline x_2,\overline x_2)$ (Remark 4.3).
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Auto.DIR

open Set

/-
It can be quite challenging to prove that a given function of several real variables is strictly
positive.
In this section we describe a simple but powerful method to automate positivity proofs for
functions on compact rectangles that we will use routinely throughout the paper. This is likely
well-known to readers familiar with computer-assisted proofs.
-/

/-! ### §4.1 Dyadic partitioning -/

/-- A *rectangle* ([DIR], §4.1): a product of closed intervals
$[a_1,b_1]\times\dots\times[a_n,b_n]\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ for $a,b\in\mathbb{R}^n$ with $a_i<b_i$
for all $i$; we use the notation $[a,b]$ (`Set.Icc a b`) for such a rectangle. This predicate
records the condition $a_i<b_i$. -/
def IsRectangle {n : ℕ} (a b : Fin n → ℝ) : Prop := ∀ i, a i < b i

/-- A *tight lower bound* ([DIR], §4.1): a map $\underline f:[a,b]\times[a,b]\to\mathbb{R}$ is a
tight lower bound of $f:[a,b]\to\mathbb{R}$ if
$f(x)\ge\underline f(\underline x,\overline x)$ for all $x\in[\underline x,\overline x]\subset[a,b]$
and $\underline f(\underline x,\overline x)\to f(x)$ as $|\overline x-\underline x|\to0$,
$x\in[\underline x,\overline x]$. -/
def IsTightLowerBound {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ xl ∈ Icc a b, ∀ xu ∈ Icc a b, ∀ x ∈ Icc xl xu, f x ≥ fl xl xu) ∧
  ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, ∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, ∀ xl ∈ Icc a b, ∀ xu ∈ Icc a b, x ∈ Icc xl xu →
    dist xl xu < δ → |fl xl xu - f x| < ε

/-- A finite partition $\mathcal{P}$ of $[a,b]$ into rectangles ([DIR], §4.1): the rectangles
$[c,d]$, $(c,d)\in\mathcal{P}$, are contained in $[a,b]$, cover $[a,b]$, and have pairwise disjoint
interiors. -/
def IsRectPartition {n : ℕ} (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (P : Finset ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ))) : Prop :=
  (∀ p ∈ P, IsRectangle p.1 p.2 ∧ Icc p.1 p.2 ⊆ Icc a b) ∧
  (⋃ p ∈ P, Icc p.1 p.2) = Icc a b ∧
  (P : Set ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ))).Pairwise
    (fun p q ↦ Disjoint (Set.pi univ fun i ↦ Ioo (p.1 i) (p.2 i))
      (Set.pi univ fun i ↦ Ioo (q.1 i) (q.2 i)))

/-- An *admissible* partition ([DIR], §4.1): a finite partition $\mathcal{P}$ of $[a,b]$ into
rectangles such that $\underline f(c,d)>0$ for all $[c,d]\in\mathcal{P}$. -/
def IsAdmissiblePartition {n : ℕ} (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (a b : Fin n → ℝ)
    (P : Finset ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ))) : Prop :=
  IsRectPartition a b P ∧ ∀ p ∈ P, fl p.1 p.2 > 0

/-
The following compactness observation reduces verification of the infinitely many conditions
$f(x)>0$ to only finitely many evaluations.
-/

/-- **Fact 4.1** ([DIR]). Suppose a function $f$ on $[a,b]$ has a tight lower bound
$\underline f$. Then $f>0$ on $[a,b]$ if and only if there exists an admissible partition. -/
theorem fact_4_1 {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (hab : IsRectangle a b) (hfl : IsTightLowerBound f fl a b) :
    (∀ x ∈ Icc a b, f x > 0) ↔ ∃ P, IsAdmissiblePartition fl a b P := by
  sorry

section Proof_Fact_4_1

/-- [DIR], proof of Fact 4.1: the 'if' part follows from the lower bound property and the
definition of an admissible partition. -/
theorem fact_4_1_if {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (hfl : IsTightLowerBound f fl a b)
    (P : Finset ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ))) (hP : IsAdmissiblePartition fl a b P) :
    ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, f x > 0 := by
  sorry

/-- [DIR], proof of Fact 4.1, 'only if' part: let $f>0$. Then for every $x\in[a,b]$ there exists
$\varepsilon_x>0$ such that $\underline f(\underline x,\overline x)>0$ for all
$\underline x,\overline x\in[a,b]$ with $x\in[\underline x,\overline x]$ and
$|\overline x-\underline x|\le\varepsilon_x$. -/
theorem fact_4_1_eps {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (hfl : IsTightLowerBound f fl a b) (hf : ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, f x > 0) :
    ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, ∃ ε > 0, ∀ xl ∈ Icc a b, ∀ xu ∈ Icc a b, x ∈ Icc xl xu →
      dist xl xu ≤ ε → fl xl xu > 0 := by
  sorry

/-- [DIR], proof of Fact 4.1, 'only if' part: the collection of $\varepsilon_x/2$-neighborhoods
around each point $x$ forms an open cover of $[a,b]$; by the Heine–Borel theorem there exists a
finite subcover, which induces an admissible partition.

**Discrepancy with the paper.** The paper's argument has a gap: the tightness condition only
controls $\underline f(\underline x,\overline x)$ for rectangles $[\underline x,\overline x]$
that *contain* $x$, but a cell of the partition induced by a finite subcover of the
$\varepsilon_x/2$-neighborhoods lies in some neighborhood of a center $x$ without necessarily
containing $x$, and $\underline f$ is not assumed monotone under inclusion. The statement is
nevertheless true: a $\delta$-fine tagged partition exists for the gauge $\delta(x)=\varepsilon_x$
(Cousin's lemma, proved by repeated bisection), and each of its cells contains its tag. The Lean
statement is the conclusion of the paper, unchanged. -/
theorem fact_4_1_only_if {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (hab : IsRectangle a b) (hfl : IsTightLowerBound f fl a b)
    (hf : ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, f x > 0) : ∃ P, IsAdmissiblePartition fl a b P := by
  sorry

end Proof_Fact_4_1

/-- **Remark 4.2** ([DIR]). It is not required that $f$ is continuous; however, existence of a
tight lower bound implies lower semicontinuity of $f$ (on $[a,b]$). -/
theorem rem_4_2 {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (hfl : IsTightLowerBound f fl a b) : LowerSemicontinuousOn f (Icc a b) := by
  sorry

/-
Admissible partitions $\mathcal{P}$ can be practically determined by {\em recursive dyadic
partitioning}, where a given rectangle is partitioned into $2^n$ congruent subrectangles called
its {\em children} by cutting along the midpoint in each of the $n$ coordinates:
-/

/-- The $2^n$ *children* of the rectangle $[a,b]$ ([DIR], §4.1): the congruent subrectangles
obtained by cutting along the midpoint in each of the $n$ coordinates. A child is indexed by
$s\in\{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}^n$ (lower or upper half in each coordinate). -/
noncomputable def children {n : ℕ} (a b : Fin n → ℝ) : List ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ)) :=
  (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin n → Bool)).toList.map fun s ↦
    (fun i ↦ if s i then (a i + b i) / 2 else a i, fun i ↦ if s i then b i else (a i + b i) / 2)

open Classical in
/-- The procedure $\textsc{Partition}_n(\underline f,[a,b],depth)$ of [DIR], §4.1, with the
argument `fuel` $=maxDepth-depth$:
```
procedure Partition_n(f̲, [a,b], depth)        ▷ depth is initially 0
  if f̲(a,b) > 0 then return {[a,b]}
  else if depth ≥ maxDepth then Fail          ▷ maxDepth < ∞
  else
    P := ∅
    for each child rectangle [a',b']:
      call Partition_n(f̲, [a',b'], depth+1)
      if not failed: add result to P
      else: Fail
    return P
```
`none` encodes **Fail**. -/
noncomputable def partitionAux {n : ℕ} (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) :
    ℕ → (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → Option (List ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ)))
  | fuel, a, b =>
    if 0 < fl a b then some [(a, b)] else
      match fuel with
      | 0 => none
      | k + 1 => ((children a b).mapM fun p ↦ partitionAux fl k p.1 p.2).map List.flatten

/-- $\textsc{Partition}_n(\underline f,[a,b]):=\textsc{Partition}_n(\underline f,[a,b],0)$
([DIR], §4.1), with the given finite value of $maxDepth$. -/
noncomputable def partition {n : ℕ} (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (maxDepth : ℕ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) : Option (List ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ))) :=
  partitionAux fl maxDepth a b

/-- The case $n=2$ of a tight lower bound, in the notation of Remark 4.3:
$\underline f(\underline x_1,\overline x_1,\underline x_2,\overline x_2)$ stands for
$\underline f((\underline x_1,\underline x_2),(\overline x_1,\overline x_2))$; i.e.
`fl x₁l x₁u x₂l x₂u` is a tight lower bound of `f x₁ x₂` on $[a_1,b_1]\times[a_2,b_2]$. -/
def IsTightLowerBound₂ (f : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (fl : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (a₁ b₁ a₂ b₂ : ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsTightLowerBound (fun x ↦ f (x 0) (x 1)) (fun xl xu ↦ fl (xl 0) (xu 0) (xl 1) (xu 1))
    ![a₁, a₂] ![b₁, b₂]

/-- The case $n=1$ of a tight lower bound: `fl xl xu` is a tight lower bound of `f x` on
$[a,b]$. -/
def IsTightLowerBound₁ (f : ℝ → ℝ) (fl : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (a b : ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsTightLowerBound (fun x ↦ f (x 0)) (fun xl xu ↦ fl (xl 0) (xu 0)) ![a] ![b]

/-- **Remark 4.3** ([DIR]). We will only use this when $n=1$ or $n=2$. In the case of $n=2$, we
will slightly abuse notation and write $\underline f(\underline x_1,\overline x_1,\underline x_2,
\overline x_2)$ instead of $\underline f((\underline x_1,\underline x_2),(\overline x_1,
\overline x_2))$. (This is the definition `Auto.DIR.IsTightLowerBound₂`; the statement below records
that it is the case $n=2$ of `Auto.DIR.IsTightLowerBound`.) -/
theorem rem_4_3 (f : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (fl : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (a₁ b₁ a₂ b₂ : ℝ) :
    IsTightLowerBound₂ f fl a₁ b₁ a₂ b₂ ↔
      IsTightLowerBound (fun x ↦ f (x 0) (x 1)) (fun xl xu ↦ fl (xl 0) (xu 0) (xl 1) (xu 1))
        ![a₁, a₂] ![b₁, b₂] := by
  sorry

/-
Running $\textsc{Partition$_n$}(\underline{f},[a,b])$ either fails or returns an admissible
partition, thus providing a proof that $f>0$ holds on $[a,b]$. Figure \ref{fig:critpartition}
[Figure 5] visualizes an example of an admissible partition that is particularly critical to this
paper.
-/

/-- [DIR], §4.1: running $\textsc{Partition}_n(\underline f,[a,b])$ either fails or returns an
admissible partition. -/
theorem partition_admissible {n : ℕ} (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (maxDepth : ℕ)
    (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (hab : IsRectangle a b) (P : List ((Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ)))
    (hP : partition fl maxDepth a b = some P) : IsAdmissiblePartition fl a b P.toFinset := by
  sorry

/-
A finite value of $maxDepth$ ensures that the procedure terminates. (In the Lean definition
`Auto.DIR.partitionAux` termination is by structural recursion on `fuel`.)
-/

/-- [DIR], §4.1: Fact 4.1 implies that if it is true that $f>0$ holds on $[a,b]$, then the
procedure will always succeed in producing a proof of this, provided that $maxDepth$ is chosen
large enough.

**Discrepancy with the paper.** Fact 4.1 only gives the existence of *some* admissible
partition, which need not be dyadic, so it does not by itself imply that
$\textsc{Partition}_n$ succeeds. The claim is true by the bisection argument (if the procedure
failed for every $maxDepth$, König's lemma gives a nested sequence of failing dyadic rectangles
shrinking to a point $x$, contradicting $\varepsilon_x>0$ from the proof of Fact 4.1). The Lean
statement is the paper's claim, unchanged. -/
theorem partition_succeeds {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (fl : (Fin n → ℝ) → (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (a b : Fin n → ℝ) (hab : IsRectangle a b)
    (hfl : IsTightLowerBound f fl a b) (hf : ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, f x > 0) :
    ∃ D : ℕ, ∀ maxDepth ≥ D, (partition fl maxDepth a b).isSome := by
  sorry

/-
(in this paper $maxDepth=12$ will suffice). In practice, limitations are imposed by memory and
time constraints.
Further limitations of the method are the requirement of \emph{strict} positivity as well as the
need for a (good enough) tight lower bound $\underline{f}$ given by an explicit formula.

The proof of positivity is established by providing the pair $(\underline{f}, \mathcal{P})$.
Verification of admissibility can, in principle, be carried out using any method of rigorous
computation, including manual computation. However, the most practical approach is to use a
computer. All necessary computations for this paper can be completed within a few seconds on a
standard laptop. We use interval arithmetic to account for all numerical and rounding errors
inherent in numerical function evaluation using floating-point arithmetic so that the bounds
produced by the computer are provably correct and this can be verified by inspection of the
source code.
-/

end Auto.DIR
